---
/**
 * Percentage calculator — 영국판 첫 도구. 한국어판 /tools/percent/ 와 계산(lib/percent.ts)·스크립트(EN 사전)를 같이 쓰고,
 * 문장은 영국 영어로 새로 썼다(번역 아님). 산술이라 나라 제도·rates 와 상관없다(조사표 영국 칸 '같음').
 *
 * 소제목은 영국 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/gb-percent-2026-10-20.csv):
 *   how to calculate percentage of a number / percentage increase·decrease formula · between two numbers /
 *   percentage off calculator · of money / percentage difference / of marks
 * title·description 은 같은 영어 판(en-us·en-au 등)과 갈리게 UK 를 넣었다.
 * VAT 숫자는 쓰지 않는다 — 영국 rates(src/rates/gb/vat-2026.json)는 아직 초안이다.
 * 이 계산기에는 percentage difference(차이 ÷ 평균) 모드가 없어서, 그 말은 질문에서 change 와의 차이만 설명한다.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../../../styles/global.css";

const title = "Percentage Calculator UK — Percentage of a Number, Increase and Decrease";
const description =
  "UK percentage calculator: a percentage of a number, what percentage one number is of another, percentage increase or decrease and a price after a discount.";
---

<html lang="en-GB">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Percentage calculator</h1>
      <p>
        Choose what you want to find, then enter two numbers. Results are rounded to 2 decimal places.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="mode">What do you want to find?</label>
          <select id="mode" data-seg>
            <option value="ratio" selected>What % of A is B?</option>
            <option value="of">What is B% of A?</option>
            <option value="change">% change from A to B</option>
            <option value="adjust">Add or take B% off A</option>
            <option value="original">Original value before a B% change</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="field" id="dir-row" hidden>
          <label for="dir">Increase or decrease</label>
          <select id="dir" data-seg>
            <option value="down" selected>Decrease (% off)</option>
            <option value="up">Increase</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label id="a-label" for="a">Whole value A</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="a" value="200" autocomplete="off" />
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label id="b-label" for="b">Part value B</label>
          <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="b" value="50" autocomplete="off" />
          <span class="unit" id="b-unit" hidden>%</span>
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="hero-value">
          <p class="label" id="hero-label">B as a percentage of A</p>
          <p class="big" id="hero-value">25%</p>
          <p class="hint" id="formula">50 ÷ 200 × 100</p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt id="receipt">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label" id="l1">Whole value A</span>
            <span class="value" id="v1">200</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label" id="l2">Part value B</span>
            <span class="value" id="v2">50</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>How to calculate a percentage of a number</h2>
      <p>
        Multiply the number by the percentage and divide by 100. 15% of 3,000 is 3,000 × 15 ÷ 100 = 450.
        To go the other way and find what percentage one number is of another, divide the part by the whole
        and multiply by 100: 45 marks out of 60 is 45 ÷ 60 × 100 = 75%.
      </p>

      <h2>Percentage increase and decrease formula</h2>
      <p>
        Take the old value away from the new value, divide by the old value and multiply by 100:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Increase</strong> — from 2,000 to 2,500 is (2,500 − 2,000) ÷ 2,000 × 100 = +25%.</li>
        <li><strong>Decrease</strong> — from 80 to 60 is (60 − 80) ÷ 80 × 100 = −25%.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Always divide by the value you started with. Going from 60 back up to 80 is a 33.33% increase, not 25%,
        because the starting point is now 60.
      </p>

      <h2>Working out a price after a percentage off</h2>
      <p>
        A price with a percentage off is the original × (1 − the percentage as a decimal). 30% off 50.00 is
        50.00 × 0.7 = 35.00, so the discount is 15.00. On a calculator, pressing <kbd>50</kbd> <kbd>×</kbd>
        <kbd>0.7</kbd> is quicker than working out the discount first and taking it away.
      </p>
      <p>
        To find the original price from a sale price, divide instead: 40.00 after 20% off was 40.00 ÷ 0.8 = 50.00
        before the sale. Adding 20% back on (40.00 × 1.2 = 48.00) does not get you to the original.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Why doesn't a 20% rise followed by a 20% cut get back to the start?</summary>
        <p>
          Because the second percentage is taken from a different number. 100 plus 20% is 120, and 20% off 120 is
          120 × 0.8 = 96. To get back to 100 from 120 you only need to take off 16.67%. The "original value" option
          shows this.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What is the difference between percentage change and percentage difference?</summary>
        <p>
          Percentage change has a direction: it compares a new value with an old one and divides by the old value.
          Percentage difference compares two values with no before and after, so it divides the gap by the average
          of the two. This calculator works out percentage change; for 40 and 60 that is +50% going up and
          −33.33% going down.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What happens if the old value is negative?</summary>
        <p>
          The calculator divides by the size of the old value (ignoring the minus sign), so a move upwards always
          shows as an increase. A loss shrinking from −100 to −50 is (−50 − (−100)) ÷ 100 × 100 = +50%. If the old
          value is 0 there is nothing to divide by, so only the difference is shown.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>What is a percentage point?</summary>
        <p>
          If an interest rate goes from 3% to 4%, it has risen by <strong>1 percentage point</strong>, but the
          percentage increase is (4 − 3) ÷ 3 × 100 = <strong>33.33%</strong>. The gap between two percentages is
          measured in points; how big that gap is compared with the starting rate is the percentage change.
        </p>
      </details>

      <AdSlot label="percent-result-en-gb" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/percent.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
